17811ed670b"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 3 + sqrt(69)/3","reason":"The small centers are at height 1 and form an equilateral triangle of side 2, whose circumradius is 2/sqrt(3). The large center is distance 3 from each small center, so its height above their plane is sqrt(9 - 4/3) = sqrt(69)/3. Adding the initial height 1 and the large radius 2 gives 3 + sqrt(69)/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_176"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_10A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_137","cnm_171"],"id":"centers-and-right-triangles","label":"Connect the centers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centers-and-right-triangles":["math:sphere-tangency-distances","cnm_176","cnm_139","cnm_265"]},"nodeIds":["math:sphere-tangency-distances","cnm_176","cnm_139","cnm_265","cnm_174","cnm_137","cnm_171"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":13,"alignmentIds":["alignment:04507dbd7ed1c17811ed670b"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 1+√(3(r²−1))","reason":"For the labeled outer intersections, let h=√(r²−1), their distance from the respective midpoints of AC and AB. Those midpoints are 1 apart. Symmetry makes B'C' parallel to BC, and each outward offset contributes a projection (√3/2)h. Therefore B'C'=1+√3h=1+√(3(r²−1)).","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_176"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":["For 1<r<2/√3, both intersections of a pair of circles lie outside the third circle, so the outside-circle wording alone is nonunique. The labeled outer intersections in the supplied figure are used.","labeled_outer_intersections_interpretation"],"questionId":"1979_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_055","cnm_250","cnm_080","cnm_096"],"id":"symmetric-coordinates-and-branch-check","label":"Compute the marked length and check the intersection choices","methodDirectNodeIds":{"symmetric-coordinates-and-branch-check":["cnm_176","cnm_133","cnm_174","cnm_259","cnm_082","cnm_084","cnm_132","cnm_140","cnm_h173"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_176","cnm_133","cnm_174","cnm_259","cnm_082","cnm_084","cnm_132","cnm_140","cnm_h173","cnm_139","cnm_055","cnm_250","cnm_080","cnm_096"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:04507dbd7ed1c17811ed670b"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 2(w + h)^2","reason":"The unfolded path from the sheet's center to a corner has length w/2 + h + w/2 = w + h. Thus the sheet's full diagonal is 2(w + h). A square with diagonal d has area d^2/2, giving 2(w + h)^2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_176"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_10B_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"unfold-and-use-pythagoras","label":"Unfold the sheet diagonal","methodDirectNodeIds":{"unfold-and-use-pythagoras":["cnm_176"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_176"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_191","cnm_055","cnm_174"],"id":"unfold-paper-diagonal","label":"Unfold a diagonal","methodDirectNodeIds":{"unfold-paper-diagonal":["cnm_176","cnm_p089"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_176","cnm_p089","cnm_191","cnm_055","cnm_174"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:04507dbd7ed1c17811ed670b"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 13 feet","reason":"During the required change of orientation, a table diagonal must become parallel to a wall, requiring S ≥ sqrt(10^2 + 8^2) = sqrt(164) ≈ 12.806. Centering the table allows rotation inside its circumcircle of that diameter. Thus 13 is feasible and 12 is not.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_176"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1988_AMC_12_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"diagonal-clearance","label":"Geometric diagonal clearance","nodeIds":["cnm_186","cnm_176","cnm_245"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_244","cnm_072","cnm_079","cnm_250"],"id":"solution:diagonal-clearance:72c627528f43","label":"Diagonal clearance and a centered rotation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"diagonal-clearance":["cnm_267","cnm_176","cnm_186","cnm_245"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_267","cnm_176","cnm_186","cnm_245","cnm_174","cnm_244","cnm_072","cnm_079","cnm_250"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:04507dbd7ed1c17811ed670b"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 2+2√7","reason":"Use box coordinates [0,4]×[0,4]×[0,h]. The large sphere's center is (2,2,h/2), and one lower small sphere has center (1,1,1). Their distance is 3, so 1²+1²+(h/2-1)²=9. Since h≥4, h/2-1=√7, giving h=2+2√7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_176"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_074"],"id":"center-right-triangles","label":"Right triangles between sphere centers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"center-right-triangles":["math:sphere-tangency-distances","cnm_176"]},"nodeIds":["math:sphere-tangency-distances","cnm_176","cnm_174","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:04507dbd7ed1c17811ed670b"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 2(w+h)^2","reason":"Following a paper half-diagonal after folding gives w/2+h+w/2=w+h. If the sheet side is L, then (L/2)²+(L/2)²=(w+h)². Its area is therefore L²=2(w+h)².","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_176"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_191","cnm_174"],"id":"folded-half-diagonal","label":"Track a half-diagonal through the folds","methodDirectNodeIds":{"folded-half-diagonal":["cnm_055","cnm_176","cnm_p089"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_176","cnm_p089","cnm_191","cnm_174"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:04507dbd7ed1c17811ed670b"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 7√21/20","reason":"The triangle has side length 3. Put s=AP and t=AQ. Reflection gives PA'=s and QA'=t. The cosine rule in BPA' gives s²=(3−s)²+1−(3−s), hence s=7/5. Similarly, t²=(3−t)²+4−2(3−t), giving t=7/4. Therefore PQ²=s²+t²−st=1029/400, so PQ=7√21/20.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_176"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1991_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_131","cnm_139","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_148"],"id":"fold-distances-right-triangles","label":"Use fold distances and right triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"fold-distances-right-triangles":["cnm_132","cnm_176","cnm_042","cnm_171"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_132","cnm_176","cnm_042","cnm_171","cnm_131","cnm_139","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:04507dbd7ed1c17811ed670b"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 62","reason":"The cosine rule in triangle BAD gives BD^2=13^2+46^2+13·46=2883, so BD=31sqrt(3). B and D lie on the circle with diameter AC, giving BD=AC sin(120°), hence AC=62.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_176"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1998_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_064","cnm_082","cnm_174"],"id":"coordinates-and-right-triangles","label":"Use the perpendicular sides to locate C","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinates-and-right-triangles":["cnm_176","cnm_140","cnm_h168","cnm_h169","cnm_h172","cnm_h173"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_176","cnm_140","cnm_h168","cnm_h169","cnm_h172","cnm_h173","cnm_064","cnm_082","cnm_174"]}]}],"canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_176"],"label":"勾股定理的应用","layer":"CN","meaning":"Practice covers aligned canonical concepts, with no claim of complete standard coverage or mastery.","nextOffset":null,"sourceNode":{"criteria":["能画图并构造直角三角形","能求实际长度"],"description":"用勾股定理解决梯子、折叠